        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES: <br>Effective exercise methods and devices for use during space flight have not yet been demonstrated.  Despite performing inflight exercise during short- and long-duration space flight, crewmembers continue to return to Earth with muscle atrophy and bone loss.  Current inflight exercise does appear to lessen deleterious effects on bone and muscle, but it does not inhibit it.  One possible missing component in current inflight exercise is an inablity to provide a load of force on the body that is equivalent to that experienced on Earth.  Previous ground-based studies have shown that lower body negative pressure (LBNP), which has historically been used in flight to maintain the cardiovascular system, produces a reaction force on the feet that can be similar to the same foot force required for the body when standing in 1-G. <br><br><p>The objective of this research is to develop and test load-bearing exercise within a LBNP chamber to maintain the cardiovascular and musculoskeletal systems in microgravity.  It is believed that this proposed countermeasure will provide a more effective mechanical stress on the lower extremity and that it will be a sufficient and comfortable method of providing a biomechanical load to maintain the structure and function of cardiovascular and musculoskeletal tissues.  It is hoped that this type of exercise device will provide a safe and economical alternative to artificial gravity in terms of power usage, mass, volume and cost. 
<p>APPROACH: <br>This ground-based study was designed to test the effectiveness of exercise within an LBNP chamber during simulated microgravity.  Lower extremity biomechanics and motion analyses during LBNP exercise within 50 to 60 mm Hg of pressure (equivalent to standing foot pressure at 1-G) during bed rest was compared to the same parameters during upright exercise in 1-G.  Groups of human subjects were exposed to 6-degree head-down tilt bed rest for 30 days.  The subjects randomly alternated between exercised and non-exercised groups, and, thus, served as their own controls. <p>Each day, the exercise group ran while supine on a vertical treadmill for 40 minutes at 50 to 60 mm Hg of LBNP pressure.  This was followed by three sets of 10 to 15 maximal leg presses at 2-3 times their body weight.  This exercise protocol was developed based upon previous studies of bone and muscle maintenance as a result of the types of mechanical loads to which the lower body is exposed. 
<p>RESULTS: <br>Orthostatic tolerance (time to pre-syncope during tilt and graded LBNP), plasma volume, and sprint speed decreased significantly (p<0.05) after 30 days bed rest in the control group, but was relatively maintained in the exercise group.  Upright VO2 peak, muscle strength of knee and spine, and endurance decreased significantly in the control group, but were preserved in the exercise group.  Also, the exercise group had normal spinal compressibility with 50% BW axial load and significantly higher back muscle strength after bed rest than the control group.  Urinary n-telopeptide (NTX) excretion, an index of bone resorption, was increased during bed rest in the control group, but not in the exercise subjects. However, markers of osteoblast activity were similar in the exercise and control groups.
<P>
The treadmill exercise protocol within LBNP maintains plasma volume, orthostatic responses, upright exercise capacity, muscle strength and endurance during bed rest.  These results document the efficacy of the exercise countermeasure in both males and females.  LBNP exercise counteracts increased osteoclast activity, but resistive exercise may be needed to increase bone formation.  Future studies should include a combination of supine treadmill exercise in LBNP and flywheel resistive exercise.  Treadmill exercise in LBNP may be an early, low mass, low power and efficacious form of artificial gravity for exploration missions.  However, the LBNP exercise hardware must be redesigned for space flight and crew habitability.<END>
